# Hub question article openings for your signature: batch 2 of 3 (S420)

You are signing the opening of each article: the words before its first heading. Each was read by a fresh checker who did not write it, and each has had the doctor and therapist lines taken out. Read what you like. Tell me the numbers you want changed or held, and say "sign the rest" for everything else.

## 13. Which CBT Techniques and Exercises Can You Try for Yourself?

It's one thing to want to change how your mind works and another to know which technique to try first. Each exercise you read about sounds sensible, but none offers a reason to start with it, so you end up feeling stuck and a little foolish. You'd simply like something practical that you can try.

Which CBT techniques and exercises can you try for yourself? Quite a number of them, and the NHS publishes a page of them written for self-help. You don't need a therapist in the room to begin practising them independently. What you need is a short list from a reliable source, and one sensible place to begin.

Afterwards you'll be able to name several techniques, explain what each one is for, and try one the same day. Each has one clear purpose, and none of them works by magic. Give one a fair trial this week and observe what changes, then try a second technique the following week if the first proved useful to you.

## 14. What Are the Most Useful CBT Worksheets and Tools?

You've been carrying a worry around, and you'd like a way to get it out of your head and onto paper. But the CBT sheets online come in their dozens, and choosing between them has become another burden on your attention. The longer you look, the less you feel like starting.

What are the most useful CBT worksheets and tools? The best place to start is a thought record. A handful of others are worth keeping alongside it: diaries that show you your own patterns, and a plan for gradually facing something you've been avoiding. That's a short list, and a single sheet of paper is sufficient to begin your first exercise.

Once you've read on, you'll know which sheet to begin with, what the others are for, and where to find good ones free. You'll also know what paper can't achieve, so you don't expect more from it than it can give. That saves you from downloading a dozen and finishing none.

## 15. Does CBT Actually Work? What the Research Really Shows

Being told again and again that something works can make you trust it less. CBT is recommended so often that you've started to wonder whether it has earned the praise or only been repeated. You're tired of sales pitches, and you'd like to be sure before you give it your time and your hope.

Does CBT actually work? The answer is yes, but only modestly. How big that yes is depends on what you're asking CBT to do, and the best evidence we have backs a careful claim rather than a sweeping one in either direction. The NHS recommends it across a wide range of conditions, though no treatment can promise you a guaranteed result.

Read on and you'll have what the largest review of the evidence found, where it's strongest and where it's weaker. You deserve a real answer, not a shrug, and a careful answer beats a slogan even when the slogan is more fun to read. With it, you can decide for yourself whether CBT is worth your effort.

## 16. Does Life Coaching Work? What the Research Really Shows

You've wanted something to change for a while, and on your own it hasn't. Hiring a coach might help, but you're wary of paying for a few kind conversations, and a bit embarrassed that you can't just get on with it yourself. Before you spend anything, you'd like to know whether anybody has checked that this works.

So, does life coaching work? The research answers carefully: studies of coaching do find real benefits on average, but they're small to moderate. Most of those studies were done in workplaces, not on life coaching as an individual buys it. The evidence is also thinner than for therapy, so nobody can promise you a result.

Once you know what the research can and can't show, you can weigh a coach's promises for yourself instead of taking them on trust. You'll also know why most of the evidence comes from workplaces, why it's thinner than for therapy, and when to see a professional first. Then trying a coach, or not, becomes your own decision.

## 17. Is NLP Dangerous, and Is It Still Relevant Today?

NLP sounds clever, even a little powerful, and the thought that it could be used on you leaves you uneasy. You don't want to dismiss it out of fear, and you don't want to take it up without a second thought. What you really want to know is whether the unease is justified.

So, is NLP dangerous? NLP isn't dangerous in the way sensational articles suggest, but it does carry real risks worth knowing about. The main one is that the field is unregulated, so almost anyone can teach it, and a title tells you little about what someone has learned. Two more risks follow: language and rapport can be used to pressure people, and some claims made for NLP run well beyond the evidence.

NLP is also still taught and read today, and how useful it is depends on what you expect from it. Knowing where the real risks sit lets you take what's useful and spot the claims worth slowing down for. You'll be able to weigh a trainer's promise for yourself and decide which parts of NLP are worth your time.

## 18. How Does CBT Compare with ACT, REBT and Mindfulness?

Every therapy you read about comes with its own set of initials, and each one can sound like the thing that works. You can't tell which are really the same, which are rivals, or which you're supposed to choose between. It leaves you feeling behind, as if you missed the page that explained it all, and not quite sure who to ask.

How does CBT compare with ACT, REBT and mindfulness? Think of them as relatives, not rivals, which is the short answer to mindfulness vs CBT. REBT dates from 1955, and the Albert Ellis Institute calls it "the pioneering form of cognitive behavior therapy", so it sits inside the CBT family. ACT, created by Steven Hayes, and mindfulness-based cognitive therapy both draw on mindfulness, and both invite you to notice a thought and step back from it.

Once you can see how they connect, the names stop competing. You'll know where each one came from, what each one asks you to do with a thought, and which questions to put to a practitioner before you choose. You'll also see where Achology starts, which is with your own thinking rather than with any technique.

## 19. What Makes a Good Life Coach, and Could You Be One?

People keep bringing you their problems, and lately you've wondered whether that means something. It's flattering, and it makes you uneasy too, because being easy to talk to isn't the same as knowing how to help. You'd like to find out for sure before you spend any money or hope on it.

Should I be a life coach? Being the person others confide in is an encouraging sign, but it doesn't settle the question. Coaching asks for a few habits that friendship doesn't always need, and you can test each one in your own life this week, using nothing more than the ordinary conversations you already have.

Four small tests will show you where you stand, which results point away from coaching, and what changes when a stranger is paying you. [What Makes a Good Life Coach?](/learn/helping-people/articles/character-traits-of-a-life-coach/) sets out the character traits behind good coaching. Nothing stops you from trying, so start by finding out how you really behave when someone brings you a problem.

## 20. Which Mindfulness Books Are Worth Reading First?

Every list of mindfulness books sounds certain, and none of them tells you which one is meant for you. You're tired of choosing badly, and wary of buying another book that you give up on by chapter three. Underneath that, you're hoping one of them might finally help you understand yourself, and not only pass the time.

Which are the best mindfulness books? At Achology, the answer depends on what you want a book for, because a shelf of mindfulness titles can be built for very different purposes. A book for looking at yourself gives you something to examine: your reasons, your choices and your beliefs. A book of ideas gives you more to look at than a book of steps, because it asks why you do what you do.

Knowing what you want a book for turns a shelf from a test you can fail into a choice you can make. You'll have three questions to put to any title and six places to start. You'll also have one question to carry into the first chapter, so the book becomes something you use.

## 21. What Are Mindfulness Skills, and How Do You Practise Them?

You react before you've decided anything, and a moment later you wish you hadn't. It keeps happening, and each time you promise yourself you'll handle it better. You're starting to feel that the reaction is faster than you are, and a little ashamed of how often you lose that race.

So what are mindfulness skills, and how do you practise them? As Achology teaches them, they're the abilities that let you watch yourself while an ordinary day is happening: what you're doing, and why. They include a pause before you respond, telling self-regulation from self-control, unpacking what people's words mean and steering your attention. You practise each one in ordinary moments, with no special place and no set-aside hour.

Seeing the space that opens up before you respond gives you a say in what comes next. You'll understand how to pause before you answer, how to tell holding back from rearranging your week, and what is pulling your attention away. None of it requires blaming yourself, and all of it begins from where you are today.

## 22. Is Mindfulness Part of CBT or DBT, or Something Else?

Mindfulness seems to belong to everything at once: therapy, CBT, DBT. You can't tell whether it's one thing or several, and nobody seems to explain the difference. You feel lost and unsure, and a little embarrassed that one word has you so puzzled while everyone else seems to follow along.

So, is mindfulness part of CBT or DBT, or something else? Mindfulness therapy is a label, not the name of one method. In programmes such as DBT, mindfulness means a set of skills taught inside a larger programme, and a leaflet using that phrase could be pointing at any of several different offers. Achology means something else by the word: turning your attention inward, to what you're doing right now and why, so you can carry more of the responsibility for your own life.

Once you can tell a label from a method, the phrase stops sounding like one thing and starts to mean something specific. You'll know where the wise mind comes from and how to use it on your own decisions. You'll also have a clear picture of what Achology means by the word, so you can set the two side by side.

## 23. What Is Stress Management, and Do the Techniques Work?

You're coping, but only just, and you can't tell any more whether you're stressed or whether this is simply what an ordinary week costs. Everything on your list seems to matter, so nothing gets your full attention. You keep telling yourself it will ease once things calm down, and you've begun to suspect it won't.

What is stress management? At Achology it means easing the two things stress is built from: too little time and too little space. You do it by deciding what deserves priority, creating room to think, and choosing what you'll take on and what you won't. One research review of workplace programmes found a medium to large average effect on the measures it gathered, and its authors name the limits themselves.

Read on and you'll see what your stress is made of, and which part of your week is short of time and which is short of room. You'll know what the WHO suggests and what one research review found, limits included. You'll also have one small step to try before the week is out.

## 24. Can Self-Awareness Be Learned, and How Do You Develop It?

It has occurred to you, more than once, that you may not know yourself as well as you assumed. The way you describe yourself sounds a little out of date, like something you decided long ago and never checked, and that leaves you unsettled. It's hard to tell whether the picture is wrong or you were simply never taught how to look.

Can self-awareness be learned? Yes, and Achology teaches it as a discipline you build by looking at yourself on purpose. It isn't a trait you were handed or denied at birth, so the habit can grow with use. The question underneath is which picture of yourself you're carrying around, and how old it is.

You build it by asking questions about how you think and act, looking at the patterns that repeat, and tracing where your beliefs came from. Read on and you'll have three kinds of question to put to yourself this evening, one about a moment, one about a pattern and one about your own history. You'll also be able to tell looking within from going round in circles.
